In Eq. (4), γ is the shear rate (s−1), k is the flow consistency index (Pa·sn), μ is the apparent viscosity (Pa·s) and n is the flow behavior index. To gather data for use in Eq. (4), the variation in sludge viscosities as a function of shear rates was measured twice at different treatment temperatures (Fig. 2
                        ), and the parameters describing the graphical relationships were also calculated (Table 1
                        ). The measured data were well described by the Ostwald model, indicating that the sludge behaved as pseudoplastic fluid.

The negative effects did not occur and thermal pretreatment accelerated HSAD significantly (Fig. 3
                        ). After thermally treated sludge was mixed with the inoculum containing soluble VFAs of 266.5 ± 15.3 mg/L, the soluble VFAs in the thermally treated sludge decreased to 600–750 mg/L (Table 2
                        ). This concentration still possibly resulted in a slight inhibition of digestion (Duan et al., 2012), but the biogas production did not deteriorate in the present study because VFAs were rapidly converted to biogas in the initial stage of digestion. When the digestion was completed, the concentration of soluble VFAs decreased to below 200 mg/L. On the other hand, the pH also increased to about 7.6 after treated sludge was mixed with the inoculum, and then pH increased further to 8.1 (Table 2) due to the consumption of VFAs and the release of ammonia. Thus, the variations in VFA concentration and pH, resulting from thermal pretreatment, did not hamper the performance of the batch HSAD.
